\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Cross-environment evidence.}
The death-avoidance finding in ScienceWorld raises a natural question: does routing signal persist in environments without catastrophic failure? An evaluation on MATH Level~4--5 (300 problems: 152 Level~4, 148 Level~5), using code interpreter as the deterministic modality, suggests not. Internal reasoning directionally outperforms code interpreter: 81.0\% accuracy (95\% CI [76.7, 85.3]) versus 76.7\% (95\% CI [72.0, 81.3]); the paired difference of $+4.3$~percentage points (95\% CI [0.0, $+8.7$]; McNemar $p = 0.079$) is marginally significant but does not reach the conventional $\alpha{=}0.05$ threshold. The label distribution inverts relative to ScienceWorld: 30 internal-preferred versus 17 deterministic-preferred (compared to 9:86 in ScienceWorld), with 84.3\% of problems (253 of 300) showing no modality preference. In the absence of death risk, the routing signal vanishes or reverses---consistent with death avoidance as the primary driver of the deterministic strategy's advantage observed in ScienceWorld.

\paragraph{ALFWorld: information asymmetry without death.}
To further test the counterfactual framework's applicability, we evaluate on ALFWorld \citep{alfworld-2021}, a text-based household environment with 6 task types (json\_2.1.1 split, valid\_seen, 135 games, max 50 steps, binary success metric), using the same Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct backbone. Unlike ScienceWorld, ALFWorld has no death penalty or graded scoring---failure means exhausting the step budget. Crucially, the two modalities differ in \emph{information access} rather than computation mode: the internal strategy receives only free-text observations, while the deterministic strategy additionally receives a numbered list of admissible actions at each step, enabling direct action selection without free-form generation. This is an information asymmetry---access to an action-space oracle---rather than the simulator mode switch used in ScienceWorld. Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling} applies without modification; with binary outcomes, labeling reduces to success under one modality and failure under the other yielding a preference, otherwise no-preference.

The overall success rate gap is large: 27.4\% (deterministic) versus 3.0\% (internal), reflecting the 7B model's near-inability to solve household tasks without admissible-action guidance. The label distribution (Table~\ref{tab:cross-env}) shows 35 deterministic-preferred (25.9\%), 2 internal-preferred (1.5\%), and 98 no-preference (72.6\%), of which 96 are both-fail and only 2 both-succeed. Among episodes where at least one strategy succeeds, deterministic is preferred at a 35:2 ratio---but this advantage stems entirely from the information gap rather than death avoidance, confirming that the counterfactual framework correctly identifies different routing mechanisms across environments. Figure~\ref{fig:signal-reversal} and Table~\ref{tab:cross-env} summarize the cross-environment comparison.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/signal_reversal.pdf}
\caption{Label distribution reversal across environments. In ScienceWorld (with $-100$ death penalty), deterministic-preferred labels dominate (86:9). In MATH (no death penalty), the pattern inverts (17:30), with 84.3\% showing no preference.}
\label{fig:signal-reversal}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Environment & $N$ & Det\% & Int\% & NoPref\% & Death? \\
\midrule
ScienceWorld & 245 & 35.1 & 3.7 & 61.2 & Yes ($-100$) \\
MATH & 300 & 5.7 & 10.0 & 84.3 & No \\
ALFWorld & 135 & 25.9 & 1.5 & 72.6 & No\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Cross-environment label distribution. Det/Int/NoPref: percentage of episodes labeled deterministic-preferred, internal-preferred, and no-preference. $\dagger$\,ALFWorld's deterministic advantage stems from action-space pruning (capability gap), not death avoidance.}
\label{tab:cross-env}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Implications for router design.}
The dominance of death avoidance in routing signal (DAF ${\sim}90\%$ at default $P{=}100$) reframes what a modality router is learning. Rather than discovering efficiency-quality tradeoffs across reasoning strategies, the router primarily learns to predict which states lead to catastrophic failure under internal reasoning. This aligns the routing problem more closely with \emph{safety critics} in safe reinforcement learning~\citep{garcia2015comprehensive} than with the cost-quality optimization framing adopted by existing routing work~\citep{cogrouter-2026, ares-2026, mtrouter-2026}. The practical implication is direct: if death avoidance accounts for the vast majority of routing value, then a lightweight death predictor---trained on state features correlated with hallucination-induced failure---may capture most of the router's benefit without the overhead of full counterfactual labeling. That the oracle outperforms always-deterministic by only 2.89 points further suggests that the residual, non-death routing signal may not justify the complexity of learning a full routing policy.

\paragraph{Label imbalance as a core finding.}
The extreme label distribution---86 deterministic-preferred, 9 internal-preferred, and 150 no-preference episodes---is not an artifact to be corrected but a central result. The 61.2\% no-preference rate indicates that for most episodes, modality choice is irrelevant: both strategies succeed or both fail. Among episodes where modality matters, deterministic is preferred at a 9.6:1 ratio, driven almost entirely by death asymmetry. This explains why always-deterministic is a strong baseline: the router's marginal value is confined to identifying the ${\sim}$4\% of episodes where internal reasoning is genuinely superior. The router training results (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) illustrate why learned routing adds no value in this regime. Step-level balanced accuracy ($54.1 \pm 5.4\%$) exceeds chance, indicating the router captures some signal from same-episode context, but episode-level accuracy ($47.1 \pm 2.2\%$) falls below chance on held-out episodes---worse than the trivial always-deterministic baseline (50.0\%). Four independent ablations confirm this is structural rather than data-limited: (i)~$32{\times}$ data scaling (25\%--800\% of original) yields no episode-level improvement (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}); (ii)~scaling router capacity from 0.6B to 4B shows no gain (\S\ref{sec:ablations}); (iii)~a task-type majority-vote baseline equals always-deterministic, since all 11 task types are deterministic-majority; and (iv)~leave-one-task-type-out evaluation reveals that 7 of 11 held-out task types have all-deterministic labels, and routing signal does not transfer across the remaining 4 (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}). The episode-level routing problem is trivially solved by always choosing deterministic: with DAF $\approx 90\%$, the routing signal that exists is overwhelmingly ``avoid death,'' and avoiding death means choosing deterministic computation for every episode. Recent concurrent work on tool-use calibration reaches compatible conclusions from the opposite direction---that LLMs may already possess implicit routing signals~\citep{when2tool-2026} but that external tool invocation carries non-trivial costs~\citep{tool-use-tax-2026}, reinforcing the case for conservative routing defaults.

\paragraph{Penalty sensitivity and generalization.}
The DAF's sensitivity to penalty magnitude---ranging from 50\% at $P{=}10$ to 98\% at $P{=}500$---highlights that death-avoidance dominance is not an invariant property of routing but a function of the environment's penalty structure. In domains with mild failure costs, non-death routing signals (e.g., reasoning quality differences on surviving episodes) would constitute a larger share of total routing value. This connects to reward shaping more broadly: the relative magnitude of catastrophic versus incremental penalties shapes which behavioral differences a router can exploit.

Our findings may also be capability-dependent. The 42.9\% internal-reasoning death rate reflects the failure mode of a 7B-parameter model prone to state hallucination. Larger models with lower hallucination rates would exhibit smaller death-rate differentials, potentially reducing DAF and revealing routing signal currently masked by catastrophic failures. Whether non-death routing signal \emph{emerges} as model capability increases---or remains negligible---is an open question with direct implications for the modality routing agenda.

\paragraph{Scope and future directions.}
Our primary analysis is grounded in a single interactive environment with a specific failure mode (the $-100$ death penalty), though the cross-environment evaluations on MATH ($N{=}300$) and ALFWorld ($N{=}135$) provide initial evidence that the counterfactual methodology generalizes: the routing signal reversal on MATH and the information-asymmetry pattern on ALFWorld both demonstrate the framework's ability to identify environment-specific routing mechanisms. Extending to environments with graded failure costs, embodied agents with continuous action spaces, or multi-turn dialogue systems would further clarify how broadly death avoidance---or analogous catastrophic-failure avoidance---accounts for apparent routing signal. Step-level counterfactual labeling, rather than episode-level, is a complementary direction that could reveal finer-grained routing structure masked by our current aggregation. A further limitation is the gap between the implicit routing signal we measure---based on episode outcomes---and the representations an implicit router would use at inference time. Our router achieves above-chance step-level accuracy ($54.1\%$) but fails to generalize to held-out episodes ($47.1\%$), and data scaling ablations show this is not resolved by more data---the limitation is structural when always-deterministic is trivially near-optimal. Whether environments with more balanced routing signal (lower DAF) would yield learnable step-level routing remains an open question. Finally, validating these findings across model scales and families beyond 7--8B parameters would establish whether the death-avoidance dominance we observe is a property of the environment or of the capability regime.

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task scatter plot: death rate differential (internal minus deterministic) vs.\ performance gap ($\Delta_{\text{full}}$). Pearson $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$). Each point is one of 11 ScienceWorld task types.}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

The full performance gap between deterministic and internal strategies across 245 episodes is $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$ points. When we exclude all episodes where either strategy results in agent death, the gap collapses to $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$. The resulting Death Avoidance Factor (\S\ref{sec:daf}) is ${\sim}90\%$ (at the default penalty $P{=}100$): approximately nine-tenths of the deterministic strategy's advantage over internal reasoning is attributable to death avoidance rather than superior task performance when both agents survive.

This concentration is consistent across task types (Appendix~\ref{app:per-task}). The Pearson correlation between per-task death rate differential and $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$; bootstrap 95\% CI $[0.952, 0.995]$), as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}. Spearman rank correlation is $\rho = 0.986$ ($p = 2.4 \times 10^{-8}$), and leave-one-out analysis shows $r \in [0.963, 0.980]$---no single task type is a leverage point (Appendix~\ref{app:correlation}). We note that death rate differential and $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ are structurally coupled through the penalty term $P$, so high correlation is expected when death dominates. The empirically non-trivial finding is that 78.9\% of non-tie routing labels (75 of 95) are driven by death asymmetry independent of $P$. Knowing how much more frequently the internal strategy dies is nearly sufficient to predict the full performance gap for a given task type. The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes compared to 16.3\% for deterministic computation---a 2.6$\times$ ratio that drives the bulk of the observed score difference.

The episode-level label distribution reflects this asymmetry: of 245 episodes, 86 are \texttt{det\_preferred}, only 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 \texttt{no\_preference}---when internal reasoning does not cause death, it performs comparably to deterministic computation.

Notably, the per-task DAF distribution is bimodal: \texttt{find-non-living-thing} exhibits DAF $\approx 0\%$ (routing signal entirely from non-death score differences, $\Delta = +9.58$), while all other task types exceed 85\%.

One task reveals the residual non-death signal. \texttt{find-non-living-thing} ($n=40$) has zero deaths under both strategies yet shows $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +9.58$, entirely from non-death performance differences---deterministic computation can provide advantages through more accurate state tracking even when agents survive. However, among the 10 death-prone task types, death avoidance overwhelms non-death differences. When both modalities survive, the gap is small but non-negligible ($\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$, 9.6\% of $\Delta_{\text{full}}$).

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-46.40$, $-32.27$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-14.71$, $-4.29$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-28.67$, $-15.75$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-11.54$, $-1.70$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router$^\dagger$ & $-10.70$ & [$-16.33$, $-5.27$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 task types, 245 episodes). 95\% Bootstrap CIs. The oracle selects the higher-scoring strategy per episode; 96.3\% of its decisions match the always-deterministic strategy. $\dagger$\,Single seed (seed 42); cross-split balanced accuracy across 8 seeds reported in \S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} reports episode-weighted mean scores and death rates for four unconditional strategies. Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points, consistent with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$. The oracle, which selects the higher-scoring strategy per episode, improves only modestly to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of its routing decisions matching always-deterministic. This \emph{oracle degeneracy} indicates that the optimal episode-level routing policy closely resembles unconditional deterministic computation---the routing signal is insufficient to improve upon simply always choosing the deterministic strategy. Random routing ($-22.38$) falls between the two pure strategies, with a death rate (27.8\%) proportional to its 50/50 allocation.

Keyword-based routing fails structurally: match rates cluster at extremes (85.8\% or $<$5\%), precluding meaningful routing discrimination.

\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

A natural question is whether the episode-level counterfactual labels can train a step-level router. We investigate this as a diagnostic probe of label informativeness rather than a routing system proposal. The negative result that follows is itself informative: it reveals the structural constraints that death-dominated routing signal imposes on any learned router, complementing the direct decomposition evidence of \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}. We test two architectures.

\paragraph{Classification head.} A linear head on LLM hidden representations, trained on episode-level labels, degenerates to always-deterministic (0\% internal recall) across all configurations (class weighting, focal loss, undersampling, temperature scaling).

\paragraph{Implicit router.} We fine-tune Qwen3-1.7B to internalize routing decisions within its generation (Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}). \emph{Step-level evaluation}---where steps from the same episode may appear in both train and validation sets---yields $54.1 \pm 5.4\%$ balanced accuracy across 8 cross-split seeds, indicating the router captures some routing signal. However, \emph{episode-level evaluation}---where entire episodes are held out---yields $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$ (8 seeds), below the 50\% chance baseline. The gap between step-level and episode-level performance suggests that the step-level signal reflects memorization of episode-specific context (enabled by same-episode leakage in the step-level split) rather than generalizable routing features. Four independent lines of evidence indicate this failure is structural rather than data-limited: (i)~a task-type majority-vote baseline achieves 50.0\% balanced accuracy (equivalent to always-deterministic, since all 11 task types are deterministic-majority), showing no task-type-level routing differentiation exists in the data; (ii)~$32{\times}$ data scaling (25\% $\to$ 800\% of original training set) yields no episode-level improvement (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}); (iii)~model capacity scaling from 0.6B to 4B shows no gain (\S\ref{sec:ablations}); and (iv)~leave-one-task-type-out evaluation shows routing signal does not transfer across task types---7 of 11 held-out types have all-deterministic labels (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}). With DAF $\approx 90\%$, always-deterministic is trivially near-optimal, leaving no learnable routing signal for a step-level router. Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness} reports per-seed results.

\paragraph{End-to-end evaluation.} Deployed as an episode-level router (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}), the trained router scores $-10.70$ (95\% CI $[-16.33, -5.27]$), not statistically outperforming always-deterministic ($-9.47$, $[-14.71, -4.29]$; overlapping CIs). The episode-to-step granularity mismatch is the bottleneck: when DAF $\approx 90\%$, router headroom is limited to the ${\sim}10\%$ non-death signal.

\paragraph{Data split.} Of 245 episodes, 95 have non-tie labels (1735/305 steps in train/validation via per-task stratified split). For step-level evaluation, steps from the same episode may appear in both partitions, introducing same-episode leakage; episode-level evaluation holds out entire episodes. Baselines in Table~\ref{tab:baselines} report trajectory statistics; the router score ($-10.70$) comes from fresh environment interaction.

\paragraph{Labeling quality.} We verify label quality in Appendix~\ref{app:labeling}.

\subsection{Cross-Model Verification}
\label{sec:cross-model}

To test whether the death avoidance pattern is model-specific, we run a mechanism verification pilot with Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on two task types (\texttt{freeze} and \texttt{find-non-living-thing}, 5 episode pairs each, $N{=}10$). Llama-8B achieves lower absolute ScienceWorld scores than Qwen-2.5-7B, reflecting weaker task-level capability, but the death pattern direction is consistent across model families.

On \texttt{freeze}, the internal strategy dies in 40\% of episodes versus 20\% for deterministic (Qwen: 55\%/25\%)---the same directional asymmetry. The single non-tie episode arises from death asymmetry: the internal strategy dies while deterministic survives, producing a det-preferred label consistent with the Qwen pattern. On \texttt{find-non-living-thing}, both strategies achieve 0\% death rate under Llama, matching Qwen exactly; the 3 det-preferred labels arise from non-death score differences.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
Task & Model & D\%\textsubscript{int} & D\%\textsubscript{det} & $\Delta$D\% & D / I / N \\
\midrule
freeze & Qwen-7B & 55 & 25 & +30 & 6 / 0 / 14 \\
freeze & Llama-8B & 40 & 20 & +20 & 1 / 0 / 4 \\
\midrule
find-non-living & Qwen-7B & 0 & 0 & 0 & 16 / 4 / 20 \\
find-non-living & Llama-8B & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 / 1 / 1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Cross-model death rate comparison. Llama pilot: $n{=}5$ episodes per task; Qwen: from Table~\ref{tab:per-task}. $\Delta$D\%: internal minus deterministic death rate. D/I/N: det-preferred / internal-preferred / no-preference labels.}
\label{tab:cross-model}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:cross-model} summarizes the comparison. This pilot ($N{=}10$ episode pairs) provides preliminary directional evidence only: the sample size precludes statistical claims. Nevertheless, observing the same death asymmetry direction in a different model family (Llama vs.\ Qwen) suggests that the pattern reflects a structural property of internal reasoning in interactive environments rather than a model-specific artifact.

\subsection{Failure Mode Analysis}
\label{sec:failure-modes}

Per-task DAF values reveal a bimodal structure: 5 of 11 task types are \emph{death-dominated} (DAF $\geq 0.95$: \texttt{inclined-plane}, \texttt{freeze}, \texttt{meas-melt-point}, \texttt{id-life-stages-2}, \texttt{grow-fruit}), where virtually all routing signal derives from death asymmetry, while the remaining 6 are \emph{genuine-deterministic} ($0 < \text{DAF} < 0.95$), exhibiting non-trivial non-death score differences alongside death effects. Notably, \texttt{find-non-living-thing} (DAF $= 0$) falls entirely in the latter category.

All 129 death events across both strategies are triggered by a single action type: \texttt{focus on <location>}. This universality is task-independent. Among internal-strategy deaths, 68\% are preceded by \emph{exploration loops}---the agent cycles between locations without task progress before issuing a lethal focus action. The top lethal locations are \texttt{workshop} (21 deaths), \texttt{inventory} (15), and \texttt{kitchen} (11). The deterministic strategy avoids these loops because its constrained action space excludes invalid navigation. All deaths occur at the terminal step of their respective episodes, which is a structural property of ScienceWorld: a lethal action immediately ends the episode. When the internal strategy dies but deterministic survives (70 episodes, 28.6\% of total), the internal agent completes only 65\% as many steps (mean 19.4 vs.\ 30).

\subsection{Ablations}
\label{sec:ablations}

\paragraph{Reasoning depth.} We compare chain-of-thought reasoning (deep internal) against direct-answer generation (fast internal) on 6 overlapping task types. The two strategies are statistically indistinguishable (Mann--Whitney $p = 0.83$, $|d| \leq 0.31$) with near-identical death rates (41.8\% vs.\ 42.5\%), confirming that the 2-way routing design (deterministic vs.\ internal) is sufficient---finer-grained internal-mode distinctions do not yield additional routing signal in this setting.

\paragraph{Router model size.} We train routers at three scales---Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen3-1.7B, and Qwen3-4B---using the same implicit-router architecture and training pipeline, evaluated via step-level generation-based classification (\texttt{enable\_thinking=False}; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}). Across four training seeds, step-level balanced accuracy is $56.4 \pm 3.5\%$ (0.6B), $58.2 \pm 4.0\%$ (1.7B), and $59.2 \pm 3.5\%$ (4B); all differences fall within seed variance. A $7{\times}$ parameter increase (0.6B $\to$ 4B) yields ${<}3$\,pp gain, well within seed variance. Combined with the data scaling ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}), which shows $32{\times}$ augmentation also yields no improvement, neither capacity nor data quantity is the bottleneck---the limitation is structural.

