36	\paragraph{Implications for router design.}
37	The dominance of death avoidance in routing signal (DAF ${\sim}90\%$ at default $P{=}100$) reframes what a modality router is learning. Rather than discovering efficiency-quality tradeoffs across reasoning strategies, the router primarily learns to predict which states lead to catastrophic failure under internal reasoning. This aligns the routing problem more closely with \emph{safety critics} in safe reinforcement learning~\citep{garcia2015comprehensive} than with the cost-quality optimization framing adopted by existing routing work~\citep{cogrouter-2026, ares-2026, mtrouter-2026}. The practical implication is direct: if death avoidance accounts for the vast majority of routing value, then a lightweight death predictor---trained on state features correlated with hallucination-induced failure---may capture most of the router's benefit without the overhead of full counterfactual labeling. That the oracle outperforms always-deterministic by only 2.89 points further suggests that the residual, non-death routing signal may not justify the complexity of learning a full routing policy.
38	
39	\paragraph{Label imbalance as a core finding.}
40	The extreme label distribution---86 deterministic-preferred, 9 internal-preferred, and 150 no-preference episodes---is not an artifact to be corrected but a central result. The 61.2\% no-preference rate indicates that for most episodes, modality choice is irrelevant: both strategies succeed or both fail. Among episodes where modality matters, deterministic is preferred at a 9.6:1 ratio, driven almost entirely by death asymmetry. This explains why always-deterministic is a strong baseline: the router's marginal value is confined to identifying the ${\sim}$4\% of episodes where internal reasoning is genuinely superior. The router training results (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) illustrate why learned routing adds no value in this regime. Step-level balanced accuracy ($54.1 \pm 5.4\%$) exceeds chance, indicating the router captures some signal from same-episode context, but episode-level accuracy ($47.1 \pm 2.2\%$) falls below chance on held-out episodes---worse than the trivial always-deterministic baseline (50.0\%). Four independent ablations confirm this is structural rather than data-limited: (i)~$32{\times}$ data scaling (25\%--800\% of original) yields no episode-level improvement (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}); (ii)~scaling router capacity from 0.6B to 4B shows no gain (\S\ref{sec:ablations}); (iii)~a task-type majority-vote baseline equals always-deterministic, since all 11 task types are deterministic-majority; and (iv)~leave-one-task-type-out evaluation reveals that 7 of 11 held-out task types have all-deterministic labels, and routing signal does not transfer across the remaining 4 (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}). The episode-level routing problem is trivially solved by always choosing deterministic: with DAF $\approx 90\%$, the routing signal that exists is overwhelmingly ``avoid death,'' and avoiding death means choosing deterministic computation for every episode. Recent concurrent work on tool-use calibration reaches compatible conclusions from the opposite direction---that LLMs may already possess implicit routing signals~\citep{when2tool-2026} but that external tool invocation carries non-trivial costs~\citep{tool-use-tax-2026}, reinforcing the case for conservative routing defaults.
41	
42	\paragraph{Penalty sensitivity and generalization.}
43	The DAF's sensitivity to penalty magnitude---ranging from 50\% at $P{=}10$ to 98\% at $P{=}500$---highlights that death-avoidance dominance is not an invariant property of routing but a function of the environment's penalty structure. In domains with mild failure costs, non-death routing signals (e.g., reasoning quality differences on surviving episodes) would constitute a larger share of total routing value. This connects to reward shaping more broadly: the relative magnitude of catastrophic versus incremental penalties shapes which behavioral differences a router can exploit.
